#
# prima-materia - the Gabonese Bouiti transmission
#
# pmt:Bwiti leaves traditions/registry-outside-contact.ttl. It was registered
# under the testability-driven entrance for one stated reason - the content of
# the experience is announced in advance - and the reading confirms the reason
# and adds the thing the registration could not know: the announced content is
# also the admission criterion, and a candidate who fails to report it is told
# that the fetish refused him and that the whole procedure begins again. That
# is a failure condition with the penalty placed on the candidate and never on
# the procedure, stated by the transmission itself, which is the material
# SPEC section 15 holds the holding is shortest of.
#
# The second finding was not looked for and is the more useful of the two.
# The earliest reachable monograph on the same people, published one year
# later from a two-year expedition, names neither the root nor the society
# anywhere in two volumes, while naming the other plant of the same
# initiation and putting it to a wholly different use. The disagreement is
# entered as pmc:WhatMelanIsDisputed and the searches that establish it stand
# in examinations/searches-bwiti.ttl.
#
# What this file does not decide. Trilles asserts that the Fang Biéri and the
# Fiote Bwiti are one thing of common origin, and this run follows the work
# rather than deciding against it: the Fang material of pages 271 and 355 and
# the Bouiti material of pages 49 to 52 stand under one tradition here because
# the opened work puts them there. Whether they are one transmission or two is
# the first question for whoever obtains Fernandez, and it is named at the
# tradition. A second question this run refused to settle by itself is a Fang
# tradition of its own: the voluntary truth-ordeal of Tessmann II, pages 241
# and 242, was read at the wording and is not an initiation, so it does not
# fall under the registered definition. It is carried here only inside the
# dispute, which needs no tradition, and the registration question goes to the
# human with a recommendation rather than being taken in a harvest run
# (SPEC sections 11 and 14, AGENTS.md).
#
# Order of the file: the five works; the tradition with the three decisions;
# the Bouiti initiation and its criterion; the two cautions; the two capacities
# and the recorder's refusal to confirm them; the Fang mélan initiation, what
# it is and what the eating of it is said to be; the éyèn mirror and the
# recorder's inspection of it; the society under its third name; and last the
# dispute about what mélan is.
#

#
# The works
#

pmw:LeTotemismeChezLesFan a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Trilles, Le Totémisme chez les Fan"@en, "Trilles, Le Totémisme chez les Fan"@de ;
    skos:note "The edition as the title page in the full text gives it: Le Totémisme chez les Fan, par le R. P. H. Trilles, C.S.Sp., avec préface de Mgr. A. Le Roy, évêque d'Alinda, Sup. Gén. C.S.Sp., Münster i. W. 1912, Aschendorffsche Verlagsbuchhandlung, in the series Internationale Sammlung ethnologischer Monographien; the signature line at the foot of page 49 gives the series number as Bibliothèque Anthropos I/4."@en ;
    skos:note "The title page spells the people Fan and the body of the book spells it Fân throughout. The work identifier follows the title page, as SPEC section 10 requires the edition to be the one read; the people are called Fang in the English literature and Pangwe in the German, which is why pmw:DiePangwe is a work about the same people under a third name."@en ;
    skos:note "Access: archive.org identifier letotemismechezl00tril, full text over the regular _djvu.txt route, 1,690,570 characters. The tail is a Princeton Theological Seminary shelf stamp after the index, so the file ends where the book ends and is not one of the truncations the access table warns about. The running heads over the parts read run 49, 50, 51, 52, 53 and 271, 272 and 355, 356 without going backwards, so the pages cited are the printed ones."@en ;
    skos:note "What was read at the wording: pages 47 and 48, the comparison of the Fang Biéri with the Fiote Bwiti; pages 49 to 52, the whole of the section Bouiti and the opening of Le Mboïo; page 271 with its first footnote; page 355 with the account of the mirror in the mvul horn. The volume runs to some five hundred pages. Not opened: the hundred and three places at which the Ngi cult is named, the chapters on the totem proper, and the Mboïo section beyond its first page."@en .

pmw:AuPaysDeSetteCamaLeBouiti a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Le Scao, Au pays de Sette Cama: les fétiches, le Bouiti"@en, "Le Scao, Au pays de Sette Cama: les fétiches, le Bouiti"@de ;
    skos:note "Named by Trilles himself in the footnote at page 49: Au pays de Sette Cama, La religion des habitants. Les fétiches: le Bouiti, par le R. P. Le Scao, 1908-1909, Messager du St Esprit. The Bouiti material of pages 49 to 52 is this article, printed by Trilles in quotation marks."@en ;
    skos:note "Not opened, and the reason is a property of the work: the Messager du Saint-Esprit is a congregational mission periodical of 1908-1909 for which no digitisation was found. Ways tried: archive.org by title and by the author name, Gallica by title and through the periodical catalogue. The article is reachable only through Trilles, which is why the nodes drawn from it carry pm:mediatedAttestation and name him with pm:readVia."@en ;
    skos:note "Trilles warns about the vocabulary of this article himself, and the warning is worth more than the usual note on a mediator: he says the study was destined for a religious review, whence certain terms and comparisons. The words Grand Fétiche, statue and secret society in the passages below are therefore the mediator's register, and the transmission's own terms survive only where the article prints them - Bouiti, Ganga, Nzimba, Diboha, Dindimba, Likoundou."@en .

pmw:BwitiEthnographyOfTheReligiousImagination a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Fernandez, Bwiti: An Ethnography of the Religious Imagination in Africa"@en, "Fernandez, Bwiti: An Ethnography of the Religious Imagination in Africa"@de ;
    skos:note "The work the registration named as principal corpus, Princeton University Press 1982, and the one that would answer the question this file leaves open about Fang and Fiote. Not opened, and the reason is a property of the work: it is in copyright and the archive copy is lending-restricted, answering 401 and 500 to the full-text route. Declared as a work of the corpus and left unopened so that queries/unopened-works.rq reports it."@en ;
    skos:note "A copy outside the lending restriction was found and deliberately not used. A work put up without its publisher's leave is not a path to the wording in the sense of SPEC section 15 - the question the access rules ask is whether a later reader can obtain the work, and an unauthorised upload answers that question for nobody."@en .

pmw:DiePangwe a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Tessmann, Die Pangwe"@en, "Tessmann, Die Pangwe"@de ;
    skos:note "The edition as the title page in the full text gives it: Die Pangwe, Völkerkundliche Monographie eines westafrikanischen Negerstammes, Ergebnisse der Lübecker Pangwe-Expedition 1907-1909 und früherer Forschungen 1904-1907, von Günter Tessmann, Verlegt bei Ernst Wasmuth A.-G., Berlin 1913, two volumes."@en ;
    skos:note "Not a work of the Bouiti corpus, and that is the finding rather than an omission. It is a monograph on the same people from a resident expedition of two years, published one year after Trilles, and it carries no occurrence of iboga, eboga, bwiti, bouiti or bweti in either volume. It is declared here because nodes cite it, not with pm:corpusWork, because pm:corpusWork says a transmission is carried in a work and this one is not."@en ;
    skos:note "Access: archive.org identifiers diepangwevlkerku01tess, 671,686 characters, and diepangwevlkerku02tess, 1,085,767 characters, both over the regular _djvu.txt route. Both tails are library catalogue stamps after the back matter, so neither is truncated. What was read at the wording: volume II, pages 241 and 242, the voluntary truth-ordeal; volume I at the three places that name Erythrophloeum guineense and volume II at its eight. The rest of some nine hundred printed pages is not opened, the Ngi cult of volume II among them."@en .

pmw:FetichismInWestAfrica a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Nassau, Fetichism in West Africa"@en, "Nassau, Fetichism in West Africa"@de ;
    skos:note "The edition as the title page in the full text gives it: Fetichism in West Africa, Forty Years' Observation of Native Customs and Superstitions, by Robert Hamill Nassau, forty years a missionary in the Gabun district of Kongo-Francaise, New York, Charles Scribner's Sons, 1904, published October 1904."@en ;
    skos:note "Access: archive.org identifier fetichisminwesta00nass, 961,833 characters over the regular _djvu.txt route. What was read at the wording: page 248, the enumeration of the secret societies of the Gabun region in chapter XV. The volume carries no occurrence of iboga or eboga. The rest is not opened."@en .

#
# The tradition
#

pmt:Bwiti a pm:Tradition ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LeTotemismeChezLesFan ;
    rdfs:label "Bwiti"@en, "Bwiti"@de ;
    skos:altLabel "Bouiti"@en, "Bouiti"@de ;
    skos:definition "The Gabonese transmission of an initiation carried by a root preparation, in which the initiate is expected to meet the dead and to report the meeting. In the earliest reachable account the report is made into a mirror set in the belly of the fetish and is the criterion of admission: a candidate who does not say what is required is told that the fetish refused him, and the whole procedure begins again."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered at places read out of pm:corpusNamed, where it stood from its registration. The registration named Fernandez 1982 as principal corpus and that work is still not opened; what was read instead is a public-domain monograph of 1912 which prints a mission article of 1908-1909 in quotation, a monograph of 1913 on the same people, and a missionary's enumeration of 1904. All three recorders are outsiders, two of them resident missionaries, and the registry file this instance leaves says in its own header that this is the condition most of its entries are in."@en ;
    skos:note "One: the contact route, and it is undecided rather than absent. The registration entered absent, and nothing had been looked for. Against a route: the vernacular terms of the procedure are not Christian, the plant is local, the society is reported under three distinct names in three languages by three recorders who do not cite each other, and nothing in the read passages has a European antecedent. For a route: the only account of the initiation is by a Catholic missionary resident at Sette Cama, written for a religious review, and the mediator says so; the fetish house is described in the vocabulary of a church, place of honour and all; and the mirror the vision is read in is a trade mirror from the coast, which the Fang account says has everywhere replaced the mica sheets used formerly, so that the instrument of the central act is an import datable to the trade. Absent would be false and documented would overstate. Until the question stands as its own node this tradition may not be counted as a party to a convergence."@en ;
    skos:note "Two: recension and edition. Trilles, Le Totémisme chez les Fan, Münster i. W., Aschendorffsche Verlagsbuchhandlung, 1912, from the title page in the full text. The Bouiti section is Le Scao's French, printed by Trilles with his own footnotes and his own bracketed additions, and the vernacular wording is not carried beside it; the Fang material of pages 271 and 355 is Trilles' own. No original-language text of any part of this transmission was read in this run, and none is printed in the works that were."@en ;
    skos:note "Three: the number of citable places. Seven, carrying sixteen nodes: Trilles pages 47, 49, 51, 271 and 355, Tessmann volume II pages 241 and 242, and Nassau page 248, all read at the wording in this run. What was left unread is recorded at each work."@en ;
    skos:note "Whether this is one transmission or two is not decided here and the decision was not this run's to take. Trilles asserts the identity - comparons le Biéri fân et le Bwiti fiot, on en verra l'identité - and derives it from a sound correspondence of r and t in Fang and from a common origin; the Fang material and the Fiote material therefore stand under one tradition in this file, following the opened work. Against the identity stands the thing the same run found: the Fang monograph of 1913 names neither Bouiti nor the root, so if the two are one transmission, the Fang branch of it is invisible in the fullest early account of the Fang. The question is the first one for whoever opens Fernandez."@en ;
    skos:note "The lease that holds this work is the branch claude/ernte-trilles-totemisme-fan, and the harvest note stands in the body of its pull request. No issue holds it, as AGENTS.md requires of an opened work."@en ;
    pm:principalCorpus "James W. Fernandez, Bwiti: An Ethnography of the Religious Imagination in Africa (Princeton: Princeton University Press, 1982); H. Trilles, Le Totémisme chez les Fan (Münster i. W.: Aschendorffsche Verlagsbuchhandlung, 1912), which prints Le Scao, Au pays de Sette Cama: les fétiches, le Bouiti, Messager du St Esprit, 1908-1909; and Robert Hamill Nassau, Fetichism in West Africa (New York: Charles Scribner's Sons, 1904)" ;
    pm:corpusWork pmw:LeTotemismeChezLesFan, pmw:AuPaysDeSetteCamaLeBouiti, pmw:BwitiEthnographyOfTheReligiousImagination, pmw:FetichismInWestAfrica ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteUndecided ;
    dcterms:source "H. Trilles, Le Totémisme chez les Fan (Münster i. W.: Aschendorffsche Verlagsbuchhandlung, 1912), pp. 49-52" .

#
# The initiation and its criterion
#

pmp:BouitiDibohaInitiation a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AuPaysDeSetteCamaLeBouiti ;
    rdfs:label "Entry into the Bouiti: three days of the Diboha root, then the mirror"@en, "Eintritt in den Bouiti: drei Tage Diboha-Wurzel, dann der Spiegel"@de ;
    skos:definition "The procedure as the article gives it. A man decided to enter the Bouiti goes to the Ganga, who fixes a date. For three days the Ganga has the postulant chew roots of a shrub called Diboha and has him drink a decoction of the same roots in repeated doses. The postulant soon grows drowsy and falls into a state of lethargy and complete absorption; he is dead drunk. A liana is then passed round his neck. Only after three days does he come a little to himself, unless he never comes back. Whoever has borne the ordeal of the Diboha more valiantly is then invited by the Ganga to look into the mirror set in the belly of the fetish."@en ;
    skos:note "The identification of the plant is the mediator's and it contradicts the one the same transmission is known for. Trilles' footnote to the word reads: Strychnos Icaja, of the Strychneae; a dangerous poison at high dose, at low dose its effects are analogous to those of indian hemp. Tabernanthe iboga is not named here, and where Trilles does name it, at page 271, it is for the Fang mélan and not for this. Whether the Diboha of Sette Cama is iboga under a cognate name or a second plant is not decided by anything read in this run."@en ;
    skos:note "The vernacular name is an optical reading and not a settled spelling. The full text gives Diboha twice, in the account and in the case of the boy at Koja. The confusions of b with h and of b with g are both common in this scan, and the cognate forms current in the Gabonese languages are of the shape diboga and dibuga, so the printed form may be Diboba or Diboga. The identifier follows the reading as the file delivers it; what would settle it is a printed copy, which is the same open item the holding already carries for pmp:Hanblapi."@en ;
    skos:note "Three days is said twice and means two different things. The Ganga administers the root for three days, and the postulant returns to himself only after three further days. Nothing in the passage says what is done with him in the interval beyond the liana round the neck, and nothing says who decides that he has borne it valiantly."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:mediatedAttestation ;
    pm:readVia "H. Trilles, Le Totémisme chez les Fan (Münster i. W.: Aschendorffsche Verlagsbuchhandlung, 1912), p. 51, where the article is printed in quotation with Trilles' own footnotes" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Bwiti ;
    dcterms:source "Le Scao, Au pays de Sette Cama: la religion des habitants. Les fétiches: le Bouiti, Messager du St Esprit, 1908-1909, the section on the initiation" .

pmc:BouitiMirrorReportDecidesAdmission a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AuPaysDeSetteCamaLeBouiti ;
    rdfs:label "He must see certain things and detail them; if he does not, the fetish refused him and all begins again"@en, "Er muss bestimmte Dinge sehen und einzeln nennen; andernfalls hat der Fetisch ihn abgewiesen und alles beginnt von neuem"@de ;
    skos:definition "The criterion of admission, and the whole of it. At the moment he looks into the mirror he must see certain things and detail them to the Ganga. If he says it rightly he is received into the Bouiti; if he does not say what is required, it is that the fetish did not want to show itself to him, refused him, and everything is to begin again. What he must see is the secret of the Ganga and of the initiates."@en ;
    skos:note "Why this node is the reason the work was opened. The criterion has the shape the holding is shortest of and the defect the holding has catalogued: the outcome is announced in advance, it is decided by one man who already knows the answer, and a negative outcome is attributed to the fetish rather than to the procedure or to the root, so that no failed run counts against either. The transmission states the excuse itself and does not leave it to be inferred."@en ;
    skos:note "What makes it worse than the excusing clauses the holding already carries is the withholding. The content of the criterion is secret, so a candidate cannot know what he is being asked and a later reader cannot check whether the same thing was asked twice. The mediator says he could get nothing out of even the converts: they are full of reticence and seized with terror at the subject, and the accounts that do circulate - that the initiate sees sometimes a goat, sometimes an elephant - are called incoherent by the man reporting them. A criterion whose content is unobtainable is unauditable by construction, and the three animals named are the one place where its content shows at all."@en ;
    skos:note "Tout est à recommencer is the operative clause and it is not a penalty. A candidate who fails the vision is not disqualified and not punished; the procedure is simply run again, which means the three days of the root are run again. Nothing in the passage fixes a number of attempts, and the only outcome the passage does make terminal is the one in the caution at pmc:BouitiDibohaMayNotReturn."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:mediatedAttestation ;
    pm:readVia "H. Trilles, Le Totémisme chez les Fan (Münster i. W.: Aschendorffsche Verlagsbuchhandlung, 1912), p. 51" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Bwiti ;
    dcterms:source "Le Scao, Au pays de Sette Cama: la religion des habitants. Les fétiches: le Bouiti, Messager du St Esprit, 1908-1909, the section on the initiation" .

#
# The two cautions
#

pmc:BouitiDibohaMayNotReturn a pm:Cautioning ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AuPaysDeSetteCamaLeBouiti ;
    rdfs:label "Only after three days does he come a little to himself, unless he never comes back"@en, "Erst nach drei Tagen kommt er etwas zu sich, wenn er nicht überhaupt nie zurückkommt"@de ;
    skos:definition "The outcome the procedure names for itself: after three days the postulant comes a little to himself, à moins qu'il ne revienne jamais - unless he never comes back. The mediator gives a case from the year of writing, a boy of fourteen who was to be initiated at Koja, who drank the Diboha and lost consciousness, and who when they tried to bring him round was nothing but a corpse."@en ;
    skos:note "The clause and the case are two different kinds of statement and the node keeps them apart. The clause is what the transmission says of its own procedure, in the same breath as the recovery; the case is the recorder's report of a death, dated to the year and placed at a named village, and it is the only outcome in these pages that anybody outside the society could have observed. Together they are the one place where this transmission names a cost it does not place on the candidate's merit."@en ;
    skos:note "It is not a condition of failure in the sense SPEC section 15 asks for. A death ends the candidate and says nothing about whether the initiation works; what would be a condition of failure is a mark by which a living initiate could be shown not to have been received, and the criterion at pmc:BouitiMirrorReportDecidesAdmission is constructed so that no such mark exists."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:BouitiDibohaInitiation ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:mediatedAttestation ;
    pm:readVia "H. Trilles, Le Totémisme chez les Fan (Münster i. W.: Aschendorffsche Verlagsbuchhandlung, 1912), p. 51" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Bwiti ;
    dcterms:source "Le Scao, Au pays de Sette Cama: la religion des habitants. Les fétiches: le Bouiti, Messager du St Esprit, 1908-1909, the section on the initiation" .

pmc:BouitiDisclosureIsCertainDeath a pm:Cautioning ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AuPaysDeSetteCamaLeBouiti ;
    rdfs:label "If they revealed anything it is certain death for them"@en, "Verrieten sie etwas, ist es der sichere Tod für sie"@de ;
    skos:definition "The sanction that holds the secret of the criterion. The initiates and even the converts are afraid to speak of what is seen in the mirror; if they revealed anything, it is certain death for them."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as its own node because of what it does rather than what it threatens. This is the clause that makes the admission criterion unauditable, and it is therefore part of the mechanism of pmc:BouitiMirrorReportDecidesAdmission and not a side remark about danger. A secret enforced by a death penalty cannot be checked by the method the rest of this holding uses, which is to ask a second witness the same question."@en ;
    skos:note "Who carries out the sanction is not said, nor whether it was ever carried out. The mediator reports the fear and not a case, which is the opposite way round from the caution at pmc:BouitiDibohaMayNotReturn, where he reports a case and the transmission supplies the clause."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:BouitiMirrorReportDecidesAdmission ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:mediatedAttestation ;
    pm:readVia "H. Trilles, Le Totémisme chez les Fan (Münster i. W.: Aschendorffsche Verlagsbuchhandlung, 1912), p. 51" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Bwiti ;
    dcterms:source "Le Scao, Au pays de Sette Cama: la religion des habitants. Les fétiches: le Bouiti, Messager du St Esprit, 1908-1909, the section on the initiation" .

#
# The two capacities, and the recorder who would not confirm them
#

pmc:BouitiSuspensionAboveTheGround a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AuPaysDeSetteCamaLeBouiti ;
    rdfs:label "Initiates of the Bouiti remain suspended a metre above the ground for more than ten minutes"@en, "Eingeweihte des Bouiti bleiben über einen Meter über dem Boden länger als zehn Minuten schweben"@de ;
    skos:definition "A capacity ascribed to initiates and reported at second hand inside the article: others affirm having seen initiates of the Bouiti remain suspended a metre from the ground for more than ten minutes. The mediator adds that still stranger things were cited to him."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because of its form and not because of its content. It is the rare case of a claim about a capacity that is quantified in both dimensions a bystander could check - a metre of height and ten minutes of duration - and that requires no initiation to observe. Almost everything else in these pages is either inside the secret or inside the candidate."@en ;
    skos:note "The counter-statement stands in the same passage and is the reason this node is worth more than the claim. The recorder writes that neither he nor his colleagues were witnesses of such facts, and gives the reason in the same sentence: that they are never admitted to these ceremonies. So the negative is explicitly not an observation of absence but a statement about access, and it must not be counted as a counter-witness. He then leaves the question open - faut-il les admettre? beaucoup le croient - and refers to a bishop's collection of analogous facts rather than deciding."@en ;
    skos:note "What would make it testable is exactly what the passage withholds. The claim is of a kind that admits a protocol, and the holding has none for it; what is missing is not an instrument but admission. No pm:Testing node is written here, because none has been carried, and the absence of one is the statement SPEC section 11 wants it to be."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:mediatedAttestation ;
    pm:readVia "H. Trilles, Le Totémisme chez les Fan (Münster i. W.: Aschendorffsche Verlagsbuchhandlung, 1912), p. 51" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Bwiti ;
    dcterms:source "Le Scao, Au pays de Sette Cama: la religion des habitants. Les fétiches: le Bouiti, Messager du St Esprit, 1908-1909, the section on the prodigies" .

pmc:BouitiSuspensionFromTheInitiation a pm:Yielding ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AuPaysDeSetteCamaLeBouiti ;
    rdfs:label "The suspension is ascribed to initiates, that is to those the mirror received"@en, "Das Schweben wird Eingeweihten zugeschrieben, also denen, die der Spiegel aufnahm"@de ;
    skos:definition "The claim that the capacity belongs to those who have been through the procedure. The passage ascribes the suspension to initiés de Bouiti and to no one else, which makes the initiation the condition of it; it does not say that the initiation is sufficient, how long after admission the capacity appears, or that every initiate has it."@en ;
    skos:note "A weak yielding claim and entered as one. What the passage supplies is an ascription to a class of persons defined by the procedure, which is the least a yielding claim can be; what it does not supply is any step between the procedure and the capacity. The holding carries stronger forms of the same shape, and this one is kept because the capacity is unusually checkable and because a reader who saw only the capacity node could not tell whether anything connected it to the initiation."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:BouitiDibohaInitiation ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:BouitiSuspensionAboveTheGround ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:mediatedAttestation ;
    pm:readVia "H. Trilles, Le Totémisme chez les Fan (Münster i. W.: Aschendorffsche Verlagsbuchhandlung, 1912), p. 51" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Bwiti ;
    dcterms:source "Le Scao, Au pays de Sette Cama: la religion des habitants. Les fétiches: le Bouiti, Messager du St Esprit, 1908-1909, the section on the prodigies" .

pmc:BouitiDindimbaStopsTheLikoundou a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AuPaysDeSetteCamaLeBouiti ;
    rdfs:label "The Dindimba liana was tied too high for a man and stops the Likoundou"@en, "Die Dindimba-Liane wurde zu hoch für einen Menschen gebunden und hält den Likoundou auf"@de ;
    skos:definition "The proof the transmission offers to an unbeliever. To convince you, they will take you to some Dindimba. A Dindimba is a liana joining the tops of two tall trees at a height where the branches appear incapable of bearing the weight of a man. Who but Bouiti could have tied this liana so high? And when the Likoundou wants to pass that way to go and eat somebody's soul, this liana stops it."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because of the structure of the argument and not because of the claim. It is the one place in the read passages where the transmission produces a physical object as evidence and names what the object is supposed to establish, and the two halves of it are of opposite kinds. That the liana is there and is high is checkable by anyone, and the transmission invites the check. That nobody but Bouiti could have put it there is an argument from the absence of an alternative, and that it stops the Likoundou is unobservable by construction, since what it stops is invisible."@en ;
    skos:note "The inference is therefore the thing to record, and the holding has the shape elsewhere: a visible warrant is produced for an invisible effect, and the warrant is genuine. Whether a man can climb that high is a question the passage treats as settled by the look of the branches, which is the whole load-bearing step. No search was made in this run for a Gabonese report of climbing to tie such a liana; the question is named here rather than answered."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:mediatedAttestation ;
    pm:readVia "H. Trilles, Le Totémisme chez les Fan (Münster i. W.: Aschendorffsche Verlagsbuchhandlung, 1912), p. 51" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Bwiti ;
    dcterms:source "Le Scao, Au pays de Sette Cama: la religion des habitants. Les fétiches: le Bouiti, Messager du St Esprit, 1908-1909, the section on the prodigies" .

#
# The Fang mélan, first hand
#

pmp:FangMelanEvomInitiation a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LeTotemismeChezLesFan ;
    rdfs:label "Eating the mélan at the evôm: a plateful of grated bark at the first ordeals"@en, "Das Essen des mélan beim evôm: ein Teller geriebener Rinde bei den ersten Proben"@de ;
    skos:definition "The Fang initiation as the recorder gives it in his own voice. At the initiation feast, the evôm, those who are to be initiated still today eat the mélan. In the initiation feasts proper, at the first ordeals, the patients are made to absorb a plateful of grated mélan bark, and that is the bark of Tabernanthe Iboga (Baillon), a shrub of one to two metres, whose alkaloid ibogaïne is soporific and aphrodisiac. In the ordeals and initiations of the second degree, under the same name mélan, the bark of Erythrophlœum guineense is used."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the only place in the read passages where Tabernanthe iboga is named, and it is named for the Fang and not for the Bouiti of Sette Cama, whose root the same book identifies as Strychnos icaja. The transmission the registration describes - an initiation carried by a root preparation - is therefore attested at the wording twice over, with two different plants, by two recorders in one volume."@en ;
    skos:note "The recorder is not a botanist and the identifications are his. He gives the authority for the iboga determination - Baillon - and gives the alkaloid of each plant and the pharmacological character of each, which is the vocabulary of the pharmacological literature of about 1901 to 1905 and not of the Fang. What belongs to the transmission in this passage is the word mélan, the name of the feast, and the fact of the eating; the rest is the identification of a European reader."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Recorded by H. Trilles, a Holy Ghost missionary of fifteen years' residence among the Fang of the French Congo, and published by him in 1912. The speaker is not named: the passage stands in the recorder's own voice as a footnote to a Fang song he prints, and he says of the custom that it is still practised today. The language of the gathering is Fang; the recorder prints the Fang terms mélan and evôm and the Fang wording of the song he is annotating, but the note itself is his French and carries no Fang sentence." ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Bwiti ;
    dcterms:source "H. Trilles, Le Totémisme chez les Fan (Münster i. W.: Aschendorffsche Verlagsbuchhandlung, 1912), p. 271, footnote 1" .

pmc:FangMelanIsTwoPlantsUnderOneName a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LeTotemismeChezLesFan ;
    rdfs:label "Two very different species furnish the mélan, and the degree decides which"@en, "Zwei sehr verschiedene Arten liefern den mélan, und der Grad entscheidet, welche"@de ;
    skos:definition "The claim the whole passage turns on, stated by the recorder as a fact about the word: two very different species furnish the mélan. The first degree takes Tabernanthe iboga; the second degree takes Erythrophlœum guineense under the same name. One name therefore covers two plants, and which one is meant is fixed by the stage of the initiation and by nothing in the name."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as its own node because the holding has nothing else of this shape and because every later claim about this transmission inherits it. A procedure named by its substance, whose substance changes with the grade while the name does not, cannot be identified from the name alone - so a report that initiates ate the mélan does not say what they ate, and two reports that agree on the word may disagree on the plant without either being wrong."@en ;
    skos:note "It is also the place where the dispute at pmc:WhatMelanIsDisputed bites. The second of the two plants is the one the Pangwe monograph of 1913 names repeatedly and puts to an entirely different use; the first is the one that monograph does not name at all. If that monograph is right about the Fang, the claim here is a claim about one plant with a second one attached, and the attachment is the recorder's."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Recorded by H. Trilles, Holy Ghost missionary, resident among the Fang of the French Congo for fifteen years, published 1912. No speaker is named; the statement stands in the recorder's own voice in a footnote, and the botanical identifications in it are his. The Fang term mélan is carried; no Fang sentence is." ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Bwiti ;
    dcterms:source "H. Trilles, Le Totémisme chez les Fan (Münster i. W.: Aschendorffsche Verlagsbuchhandlung, 1912), p. 271, footnote 1" .

pmc:FangMelanHighDoseIsDeath a pm:Cautioning ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LeTotemismeChezLesFan ;
    rdfs:label "At low dose a kind of ecstatic drunkenness, at high dose death"@en, "In geringer Dosis eine Art ekstatischer Rausch, in hoher Dosis der Tod"@de ;
    skos:definition "The dose statement the recorder attaches to the mélan: it causes at low dose a kind of ecstatic drunkenness and at high dose death. Of the second plant he adds that it contains a dangerous poison, erythrophlœine, which provokes convulsions followed by arrest of the heart, and that the same tree serves as an ordeal poison in the Casamance under the names deli and tell, as a related species does in Madagascar."@en ;
    skos:note "The dose relation is the thing to keep. What the procedure wants is the ecstatic drunkenness, and the quantity that produces it is on the same axis as the quantity that kills, with no mark given between them. A procedure whose intended effect and whose lethal effect differ only in dose has a cost that falls on the practitioner and not on the claim, and in this transmission the cost is attested twice: here as a general statement and at pmc:BouitiDibohaMayNotReturn as a named case."@en ;
    skos:note "The ordeal use in the Casamance and in Madagascar is the recorder's comparative apparatus and not a Fang claim, and it is kept out of the definition's first sentence for that reason. It matters nonetheless, because the same plant turns up as an ordeal poison in the Fang monograph of 1913, and the recorder here records the ordeal use elsewhere while not reporting it for the Fang."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:FangMelanEvomInitiation ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Recorded by H. Trilles, Holy Ghost missionary resident among the Fang of the French Congo for fifteen years, published 1912, in his own voice in a footnote. No speaker is named. The pharmacological terms are the recorder's; the Fang wording is not carried." ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Bwiti ;
    dcterms:source "H. Trilles, Le Totémisme chez les Fan (Münster i. W.: Aschendorffsche Verlagsbuchhandlung, 1912), p. 271, footnote 1" .

pmc:FangEatingTheMelanIsBeingInitiated a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LeTotemismeChezLesFan ;
    rdfs:label "I have eaten the mélan and am consecrated with the priests of the night"@en, "Ich habe den mélan gegessen und bin mit den Priestern der Nacht geweiht"@de ;
    skos:definition "The line of the invocation to the Thunder that the footnote on the mélan annotates: O Thunder, O Thunder, Father and Lord, Lord and Father, hear favourably the prayers and receive the gifts. I have eaten the mélan - and the recorder glosses the clause in parenthesis, that is, I am initiated - and I am consecrated with the priests of the night."@en ;
    skos:note "The equation of the act with the status is the recorder's gloss and not a separate clause of the song, and the node says so rather than carrying the gloss as if the song said it. What the song says is that the speaker has eaten the mélan and is consecrated; that eating it is what being initiated consists in is the annotation of the man who printed it."@en ;
    skos:note "The recorder draws a dating conclusion from the same song and it is worth recording as his, because it is an argument of a kind the holding should be able to see. He says the song proves the antiquity of these customs, and his ground is given a few lines above: the archaic terms of the song in part escape the comprehension of the natives today. That is an inference from unintelligibility to age, made by the compiler, and nothing in the passage supports or dates it further."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "A Fang narrator's recitation at an assembly, taken down by H. Trilles among the Fang of the French Congo and published by him in 1912 in French translation. The narrator is not named and the Fang wording of the invocation is not printed beside the translation; the recorder says the refrain is taken up by the assembly and that a long song follows whose archaic terms the hearers no longer fully understand." ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Bwiti ;
    dcterms:source "H. Trilles, Le Totémisme chez les Fan (Münster i. W.: Aschendorffsche Verlagsbuchhandlung, 1912), p. 271" .

#
# The éyèn mirror, and the recorder's inspection of it
#

pmp:FangEyenMirrorConsultation a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LeTotemismeChezLesFan ;
    rdfs:label "Looking into the éyèn in the mvul horn at the figures that pass"@en, "Der Blick in das éyèn im mvul-Horn auf die Gestalten, die vorbeiziehen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The practice the recorder reveals as the secret of the mvul, the great antelope horn that is with the bones of ngure and mvi the most important part of the akaméyôn. The end of the mvul is closed by a mirror, éyèn or éyèna. It is in this mirror that the chief, after the prescribed ceremonies and rites, looks at the figures that pass, whether the matter is an initiation, or the discovery of thieves, or any other hidden fact. Inside the horn are a fragment of the skull of one of the ancestors, a fragment of the mvamayôn, ashes from the sacrifice, and nail parings, hair and dried blood and mucus of the bearer himself."@en ;
    skos:note "The practice belongs to the same complex as the Bouiti mirror and is reported by the other of the two recorders, in his own voice and for the other people. In both the vision is read in a mirror set in a fetish, in both an initiation is one of the occasions, and in both the mirror is the instrument of the decision. Whether that makes two reports of one procedure or one procedure and a parallel is the question the tradition note leaves open."@en ;
    skos:note "The recorder says of the secret that the chief Esôya had not revealed it to the earlier author he names, and that he is giving it here; and he declines to go into the mirror itself on the ground that it is of a purely fetishistic order and not his subject. So the fullest statement in the book about the instrument of the vision is made in passing and against the grain of the chapter it stands in."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Recorded by H. Trilles among the Fang of the French Congo, published 1912, in his own voice. The informant for the content of the horn is not named; the chief Esôya is named as the man who had withheld the secret from an earlier author. The Fang terms mvul, éyèn, éyèna, akaméyôn and mvamayôn are carried; no Fang sentence is. The recorder adds an observation of his own: he was able to procure only a single specimen of the mica sheets the trade mirrors have everywhere replaced." ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Bwiti ;
    dcterms:source "H. Trilles, Le Totémisme chez les Fan (Münster i. W.: Aschendorffsche Verlagsbuchhandlung, 1912), p. 355" .

pmc:FangEyenShowsWhatIsHidden a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LeTotemismeChezLesFan ;
    rdfs:label "The éyèn shows an initiation, a thief, or any other hidden fact"@en, "Das éyèn zeigt eine Einweihung, einen Dieb oder jede andere verborgene Sache"@de ;
    skos:definition "The capacity ascribed to the mirror: it serves in very many circumstances and great confidence is placed in it, and what it is held to show is an initiation, the identity of thieves, or any other hidden fact."@en ;
    skos:note "The recorder's inspection of the instrument stands in the same sentence as the confidence placed in it, and it is the sharpest counter-observation in the passages read. He writes that most of the time this mirror is in such bad condition or so frightfully tarnished that only the imagination, violently overexcited by the absorption of certain substances or vegetable alkaloids - he names ibogaïne, ouabaïne, strophantine - can permit anything at all to be seen in it. The claim and the state of the thing the claim runs on are therefore both attested by the same witness, and he is a witness who handled the objects."@en ;
    skos:note "Why that is not entered as a dispute and not as a protocol. It is one source, so pm:Disputing cannot hold it; and no procedure was devised and no case counted, so pm:Testing would be a stub of the kind SPEC section 11 forbids. What it is, is a condition under which the claim would be shown to be about something other than the mirror - that the mirror cannot carry an image - and that condition is met, by the recorder's own report, most of the time. Whoever writes the protocol has the falsifying condition already stated and needs a count."@en ;
    skos:note "The trade mirror is the second finding at this place and it bears on the contact route. The Fang obtain mirrors easily from the little pomade boxes covered with a glass that are sold in quantity on the coast, and these have everywhere replaced the mica sheets formerly used. The instrument of the central act is therefore an import of the trade era, and the practice as described cannot be older than the glass in it, whatever is true of the practice that used mica."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Recorded by H. Trilles among the Fang of the French Congo, published 1912, in his own voice, with no informant named for the claim. The recorder's own inspection of the mirrors and his procuring of a single mica specimen are reported by him as his own observation and are kept distinct from the ascription in the notes above. The Fang term éyèn is carried; no Fang sentence is." ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Bwiti ;
    dcterms:source "H. Trilles, Le Totémisme chez les Fan (Münster i. W.: Aschendorffsche Verlagsbuchhandlung, 1912), p. 355" .

pmc:FangEyenVisionFromTheConsultation a pm:Yielding ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LeTotemismeChezLesFan ;
    rdfs:label "The figures pass after the prescribed ceremonies and rites"@en, "Die Gestalten ziehen vorbei nach den vorgeschriebenen Zeremonien und Riten"@de ;
    skos:definition "The claim that the vision follows the procedure: the chief looks and the figures pass après les cérémonies et rites prescrits. The ceremonies are prescribed and are the condition; which they are is not said at this place, and the chapter the passage stands in gives the consecration of the other parts of the akaméyôn but not of the mirror."@en ;
    skos:note "The yielding claim is entered separately from the capacity because the condition is the only part of it the passage states, and because the recorder's inspection at pmc:FangEyenShowsWhatIsHidden tells against the capacity and not against this. A reader who had only the capacity node would not see that the transmission makes the vision conditional on anything."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:FangEyenMirrorConsultation ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:FangEyenShowsWhatIsHidden ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Recorded by H. Trilles among the Fang of the French Congo, published 1912, in his own voice, no informant named. The Fang wording is not carried." ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Bwiti ;
    dcterms:source "H. Trilles, Le Totémisme chez les Fan (Münster i. W.: Aschendorffsche Verlagsbuchhandlung, 1912), p. 355" .

#
# The society under a third name
#

pmc:BwetiAmongTheBakele a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:FetichismInWestAfrica ;
    rdfs:label "Among the Bakele, Bweti, named as one of the governing societies of the Gabun region"@en, "Bei den Bakele Bweti, genannt als eine der regierenden Gesellschaften der Gabun-Region"@de ;
    skos:definition "The whole of what the third recorder says of the society. In the Gabun region of the equator there is, among the Shekani, Mwetyi; among the Bakele, Bweti; among the Mpongwe-speaking tribes, Inda and Njembe; and Ukuku and Malinda in the Batanga regions. He names them among the societies whose primary object was the good one of government, holding the fetich in terror, and which in one aspect of their influence were before the intrusion of foreign powers the only authority that could settle a fierce personal dispute or enforce intertribal peace."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because the name is the only thing it confirms, and that is worth having precisely. A missionary of forty years' residence in the same region, writing in English and citing neither of the French recorders, puts the society in the Gabun region under the form Bweti and assigns it a people - the Bakele, a third people, neither the Fang of Trilles nor the Bavili of Sette Cama. Nothing of the initiation, nothing of the root, and no occurrence of iboga anywhere in the volume."@en ;
    skos:note "What it is therefore evidence of, and what it is not. It is independent evidence that a society of this name existed in this region before 1904 and was taken to be an instrument of government. It is no evidence at all about the initiation, the root, the mirror or the criterion, and it must not be counted as a second witness to any of them. The recorder gives a detailed account of an initiation elsewhere in the book, and it is an initiation into Malinda and not into Bweti."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Recorded by Robert Hamill Nassau, an American Presbyterian missionary of forty years' residence in the Gabun district, in English, published 1904. No informant is named for the enumeration; the recorder states elsewhere in the same chapter that he came into contact with two of the societies he lists himself. The vernacular names are given in his own transcription and no vernacular sentence is carried." ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Bwiti ;
    dcterms:source "Robert Hamill Nassau, Fetichism in West Africa: Forty Years' Observation of Native Customs and Superstitions (New York: Charles Scribner's Sons, 1904), p. 248" .

#
# The dispute, and it is the finding of the run
#

pmc:WhatMelanIsDisputed a pm:Disputing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LeTotemismeChezLesFan, pmw:DiePangwe ;
    rdfs:label "Whether the mélan of the Fang initiation is iboga, and whether the Fang had the society at all"@en, "Ob der mélan der Fang-Einweihung Iboga ist, und ob die Fang die Gesellschaft überhaupt hatten"@de ;
    skos:definition "A disagreement between two monographs on the same people published one year apart. One says that two species furnish the mélan, that the first ordeals use the bark of Tabernanthe iboga and the second degree that of Erythrophlœum guineense, and that the Fang Biéri is the same thing as the Fiote Bwiti. The other, from a resident expedition of 1907 to 1909 with earlier work from 1904, names Erythrophlœum guineense repeatedly under the Pangwe name elün, calls it the poison tree, and gives it a use that is not an initiation at all: the custom of the voluntary proof of truth without invocation of any moral power, in which a person who believes himself falsely accused chews the poisonous bark with water three times and publicly declares his innocence, the opinion being that in real innocence the poison is vomited up again, and otherwise stays in the body and acts fatally. In two volumes of that monograph the words iboga, eboga, bwiti, bouiti and bweti do not occur."@en ;
    skos:note "What is at issue and what is not. Both works agree that Erythrophlœum guineense is in Fang hands and is deadly. They disagree about what the first ordeals of the initiation use, about whether the people had a society of this name, and about what the plant is for: a graded initiation in one, a judicial ordeal undertaken by the accused in the other. Neither contradicts the other on the Bouiti of Sette Cama, which lies outside the second recorder's region."@en ;
    skos:note "Why silence counts here and not at pmc:BouitiSuspensionAboveTheGround. There the recorder gave a reason for his own ignorance that is a property of access - he was never admitted. Here the second recorder was admitted to a great deal, gives the men's cults of the people by name over many pages, lists the plants of the region with their Pangwe names and their Linnean ones, and names this plant eleven times; a transmission he does not mention under any of its five spellings is not a transmission he was kept away from. The silence is therefore a finding about the corpus and is recorded as such at pmp:PangweIbogaSearch."@en ;
    skos:note "Three readings are open and the holding decides none of them. That the Fang branch of the transmission is later than 1913, which is what the second monograph would imply and which would make the first recorder's identification of Biéri with Bwiti a claim about the Fiote projected onto the Fang. That the first recorder had the plant wrong and the mélan of the Fang is the Erythrophlœum alone, in which case his two species are one species with a European identification attached. Or that the second recorder's informants withheld it, which his own method makes harder to assert than it would be of a traveller. What would decide it is the work this file leaves unopened."@en ;
    skos:note "The ordeal claim in the second work is of the form this project values most and it does not belong to this tradition. Innocence is to produce vomiting and guilt death, which is a stated criterion with two observable outcomes and the penalty on the person; the recorder adds, as his own explanation and not as the transmission's, that people who believe the accusation drive the accused into the state they call olü'n until he either kills himself or ends the talk by undertaking the proof. The ordeal is not an initiation and falls outside the registered definition of this tradition, so it is carried here only as the second side of the dispute, and the question whether a Fang tradition of its own is to be registered goes to the human with a recommendation."@en ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:FangMelanIsTwoPlantsUnderOneName ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    dcterms:source "H. Trilles, Le Totémisme chez les Fan (Münster i. W.: Aschendorffsche Verlagsbuchhandlung, 1912), p. 271, footnote 1, with the identity of Biéri and Bwiti asserted at pp. 47 and 49", "Günter Tessmann, Die Pangwe (Berlin: Ernst Wasmuth A.-G., 1913), vol. II, pp. 241-242" .

